  and an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m. There is no task model, no JavaScript, no users, no server, no build tooling, no tests. Nothing
  to attach notifications to yet.

  That matters more than it might sound. "Users get notified when tasks they care about change" implies at least three
  things this repo doesn't have: a notion of a user, a notion of a task that changes, and something that persists across
  sessions so a change made by one party reaches another. A notification system is the [3mlast[0m layer of that stack, not
  the first.

  So the fork I need to resolve before anything else:

  - [1mThis repo is the whole app[0m — then notifications aren't really the task; building the task app with notifications as
    one feature is. That's a decomposition conversation: task model and storage first, then multi-user/identity, then
    notifications on top. Each gets its own spec.
  - [1mThe real app lives elsewhere[0m — a backend, another repo, an existing deployment — and this file is a stub or a
    fixture. Then I need to see that code before designing anything, because the notification design is almost entirely
    determined by what already exists there.
  - [1mYou want a self-contained front-end-only version[0m — notifications for changes within a single browser session or
    local storage, no server, no other users. That's a much smaller and genuinely buildable thing, but "users" then
    means "one user, one browser."

  Which of these is the actual situation?
